---
/**
 * How to hold any of the four portfolios: what to do before investing, which account
 * each fund goes in (with the calculator), how to buy, when to rebalance, what to do
 * when it falls, what to do if RSST closes, and the broker fee to check for.
 *
 * Every number that describes what a portfolio did is read from the research emitter's
 * series at build time. The worked purchase example is the Plus trend weights on $10,000.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import PlacementTool from "~/components/islands/PlacementTool";
import { PORTFOLIOS, WITH_TREND_HOLDINGS } from "~/content/portfolios";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { formatDollars, formatPct } from "~/lib/charts";
import { loadSeries, seriesFor } from "~/lib/series";

const file = loadSeries("1990");
const market = seriesFor("market");

/** What each portfolio, and the plain index, did in 2008 and 2022, and its worst fall. */
const FALLS = [...PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => seriesFor(p.slug)), market].map((s) => {
  const y2008 = s.summary.calendarYears["2008"];
  const y2022 = s.summary.calendarYears["2022"];
  if (y2008 === undefined || y2022 === undefined) throw new Error(`${s.id} is missing a calendar year`);
  const months = s.summary.worstFall.monthsToRecover;
  return {
    id: s.id,
    label: s.id === "market" ? "A plain world stock index" : s.label,
    y2008,
    y2022,
    y2008Text: formatPct(y2008),
    y2022Text: formatPct(y2022),
    trough: formatDollars(s.summary.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough, "exact"),
    months: months === null ? "not yet" : String(months),
  };
});

const plain = FALLS[FALLS.length - 1];
if (plain === undefined) throw new Error("no series to print");
const four = FALLS.filter((row) => row.id !== "market");
/** The smallest and largest fall among the four portfolios in a calendar year, as positive percents. */
function range(year: "y2008" | "y2022"): [string, string] {
  const falls = four.map((row) => -row[year]);
  return [formatPct(Math.min(...falls)), formatPct(Math.max(...falls))];
}
const [least2008, most2008] = range("y2008");
const [least2022, most2022] = range("y2022");

/** The Plus trend weights as dollars of a $10,000 purchase. */
const EXAMPLE = WITH_TREND_HOLDINGS.map((h) => `${formatDollars(h.weight * 100, "exact")} of ${h.ticker}`);
const exampleText = `${EXAMPLE.slice(0, -1).join(", ")} and ${EXAMPLE[EXAMPLE.length - 1]}`;

// TODO(fact): FACTS.md has no line on employer matches. The "Before you invest" sentence
// about a match states no number and rests on arithmetic alone; add a sourced line or cut it.
---

<Base
  title="How to hold it"
  description="Which account each fund goes in, how to buy, when to rebalance, what to do when the portfolio falls, and what to do if RSST closes."
>
  <Hero
    title="How to hold it"
    lede="Pick one of the four portfolios, then hold it the same way whatever the market does. This page is the whole routine: which account, how to buy, once-a-year upkeep, and what to do when it falls."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Everything here about tax is US federal tax. State tax adds on top, and outside the US every line
    differs.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="before-you-invest">Before you invest</h2>

    <p>If you have debt at a rate above what a US government bond pays after tax, pay it down first. Repaying it
    is a return with no risk, and very likely the best one available to you. The one cost: a cheap fixed-rate
    mortgage, once repaid, cannot be borrowed again at that rate.</p>

    <p>If your employer matches part of what you put into its retirement plan, put in at least enough to get the
    whole match. A dollar matched is a dollar earned on the day you deposit it.</p>

    <p>If you have a high-deductible health plan, fill the health savings account before a taxable account. It is
    the only US account taxed at none of the three points: money in, growth, and money out for medical costs.
    The 2026 limit is $4,400 for one person and $8,750 for a family, plus $1,000 from age 55.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="which-account">Which account</h2>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>If all your money is in one account, skip this section. Buy the funds in the weights shown and go to
      <a href="#rebalancing">Rebalancing</a>.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>If you have a retirement account and a taxable brokerage account, the rule is two lines. Foreign stock
    funds and RSST go in the retirement account; plain US stock funds, VTI and VTV, go in the taxable account.
    The bond fund goes in the retirement account before anything else.</p>

    <p>The reason is the yearly tax bill. In a taxable account you pay tax every year on what a fund pays out; in
    a retirement account you pay none, so the fund with the biggest yearly bill goes there first. Bond interest
    is taxed like wages, so the bond fund's bill is the largest: about $190 a year on every $10,000 of it at the
    top bracket. RSST's trend profits are taxed like wages too. Foreign funds pay bigger dividends than US funds,
    and fewer of them are dividends taxed at the lower rate. VTI pays the smallest dividend, all of it at the
    lower rate, so it costs the least to leave in the taxable account.</p>

    <p>You may have read that foreign funds belong in a taxable account, to keep the foreign tax you can claim
    back. Holding all four in a retirement account does lose that claim, about $7 a year on every $10,000 of the
    whole portfolio, and saves several times that.</p>

    <p>If you have both a Roth and a pre-tax account, RSST and the bond fund go in the pre-tax account and the
    foreign funds in the Roth: the Roth is never taxed again, so give it what you expect to grow most. That
    choice is worth about $2 a year per $10,000; taxable against retirement is worth ten times that.</p>

    <p>RSST records income inside itself that it has not paid out. I count all of it, which puts RSST first in
    the queue; it belongs in a retirement account either way. The calculator ranks the eight funds of the Plus
    trend portfolio at your bracket and says where each one lands. Nothing you set leaves your browser.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <PlacementTool client:visible />
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <p>Doing this is worth about $2 to $7 a year on every $10,000 against a sensible default with the same
    accounts, and nothing if all your money is in one kind of account. If your pre-tax money is all in an
    employer plan whose menu lacks these funds, rolling an old employer balance into a rollover IRA is the
    cheapest single improvement available.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="how-to-buy">How to buy</h2>

    <p>Open a brokerage account; Fidelity, Schwab and Vanguard charge nothing to trade ETFs online. Buy each fund
    by its ticker in the weights shown. As a worked example, $10,000 in Plus trend is
    <span data-numeric>{exampleText}</span>. Whether to buy all at once or a bit each month, and whether to buy
    whole or fractional shares, has not been measured here; do whichever you will keep doing. New money goes to
    the accounts with yearly limits first, a 401(k) or an IRA, then to the taxable account, into whatever
    holding is furthest below its target.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="rebalancing">Rebalancing</h2>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Once a year, on a fixed date, compare each holding with its target across all your accounts together.
      Send new money and any dividends to whatever is furthest below target. Trade only inside a retirement
      account, and only when a holding is a quarter away from its target. Never sell in a taxable account to
      rebalance.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>Rebalancing keeps your mix, and that is all it does. Do not expect it to add return or soften the worst
    fall: on this portfolio the yearly rule came out 0.09% a year behind never trading over
    {file.window.label}, a difference the test cannot tell from zero. The quarter-away trigger fires about once
    every two and a half years. Never selling in the taxable account is worth about $42 a year on $10,000
    against selling a tenth of your holdings each year, because tax on a gain waits until you sell.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="when-it-falls">When it falls</h2>

    <p>The whole site rests on one choice: pick the fall you can sit through, then sit through it. Sitting
    through it looks like this. In 2008 the four portfolios fell between
    <span data-numeric>{least2008}</span> and <span data-numeric>{most2008}</span> in one year, and in 2022
    between <span data-numeric>{least2022}</span> and <span data-numeric>{most2022}</span>. At the worst point,
    $10,000 in the plain world stock index was worth <span data-numeric>{plain.trough}</span> and took
    <span data-numeric>{plain.months}</span> months to get back to $10,000. Every number is a simulation from
    index data, before tax, over {file.window.label}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 max-w-measure overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" aria-label="What each portfolio did in 2008 and 2022">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm sm:text-base">
      <caption class="sr-only">Each portfolio's return in calendar 2008 and 2022, what $10,000 was worth at the bottom of its worst fall, and the months it took to recover, {file.window.label}, simulated</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 font-semibold">Portfolio</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 text-right font-semibold">2008</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 text-right font-semibold">2022</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-2 text-right font-semibold">$10,000 at the bottom</th>
          <th scope="col" class="label py-2 text-right font-semibold">Months back to even</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-ink-2">
        {FALLS.map((row) => (
          <tr class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
            <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-2 text-left font-medium text-ink">{row.label}</th>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.y2008Text}</td>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.y2022Text}</td>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.trough}</td>
            <td data-numeric class="py-2 text-right whitespace-nowrap">{row.months}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Do not sell. Keep adding on the same schedule. Before you buy, write down the fall you could sit through
      and how many years behind a plain index fund you could stand, and hold yourself to the note. A price never
      reopens the choice; only a change in the evidence or a change in you does.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>Falling behind a plain US index fund for years is also inside what these portfolios have done.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="if-rsst-closes">If RSST closes</h2>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>If RSST announces it is closing or changing what it does, sell it before its last trading day and buy
      VTI with the money in the same retirement account. No tax is due there, and you now hold the value lean.
      Do not replace it with a standalone trend-following fund bought by selling stocks.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>Closing is a real possibility: of the 25 trend-following ETFs filing reports in mid-2019, 13 had stopped
    by the end of 2025. Four possible replacements (CTAP, MATE, JPFP, RSIT) are reviewed in that order, and each
    currently fails a test; the <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">Plus trend page</a> will say when one passes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="broker-fees">Broker fees</h2>

    <p>Since 1 June 2026 Fidelity charges 5% of the trade, capped at $100, to buy ETFs from issuers that refuse
    to pay its platform fee. Its list as of 15 August 2026 names 84 tickers and none of the funds on this site,
    but it can change without notice, and Schwab has announced a similar programme for late 2026 to early 2027.
    Small issuers, Return Stacked among them, are the ones plausibly at risk.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Before a first purchase, search your broker's site for its list of ETFs that carry a purchase fee and
      check every ticker you plan to buy. If a fund you hold is ever listed, buy it once or twice a year and send
      the monthly money to the index funds: $100 on a $10,000 purchase is 1% on day one, above a year's fee of
      every fund here except RSST.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Base>
